{-
   Time, as a point and as a duration - Analysis-Library.md §3.1.

   Two newtypes over an `I64` of microseconds, and the reason there are two rather than one is the
   whole design: `Timestamp` is a *point* on a line and `Interval` is a *displacement* along it, and
   the operations that are legal between them are not the ones a single numeric type would offer.
   Subtracting two points gives a displacement; adding a displacement to a point gives a point; and
   adding two points is not an operation, in the way that adding two dates on a calendar is not one.

   **Neither type has a `Num` instance, and that is a decision this file exists to record.**
   `Num(a)` is single-typed - `+ : (a, a) -> a` - so the affine structure above does not fit it.
   Instancing it for `Timestamp` would admit `Timestamp + Timestamp`, which is the one thing the
   split is for. What is written instead is three small classes - `Advance`, `Difference` and
   `Scale` - covering seven operations between them, which is the alternative §3.1 names in the same
   sentence and sets aside. It sets it aside in favour of free operators, and free operators turn out
   not to be available: see the section below, which is the one place this file departs from what
   §3.1 asked for and says why it had to.

   **`Interval` has no `FromInt` instance either**, for the same reason read from the other end:
   `let t: Interval = 5` reads as neither five seconds nor five microseconds. The C++'s `0.5_s` and
   `100_ms` literal suffixes have no counterpart here, and `seconds(3)` and `millis(500)` are the
   better spelling anyway - a unit named in the call rather than glued to the number.

   Microseconds and not nanoseconds, which is the range decision the C++ made and it carries over:
   a signed 64-bit nanosecond count runs out in the year 2262, and a microsecond one covers about
   292 thousand years either side of the epoch. What is left needing nanoseconds is measuring a short
   operation, and that is `monotonicNanos` below, which deliberately does not answer an `Interval`.

   `DateFormat` is not here. It is the other half of §3.1 and it is a text-shaped problem rather
   than a time-shaped one - a template language, its parser and a formatter - so it is left for its
   own file, and nothing below anticipates it.
-}

import Native

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The two types.
   ==========================================================================================

   `Eq` and `Ord` are derived rather than written, which for a newtype over an integer is the whole
   of what a hand-written instance would say. `Show` is not derivable here: it would forward to
   `show` on the *content*, and `I64` is not in `Show` - the instances are at `Int` and `Long`, the
   two canonical widths - so the one-line forward is written out below instead.

   Comparing two `Interval`s is meaningful and so is ordering them, which is why `Ord` is on both.
   What is not meaningful is comparing an `Interval` to a `Timestamp`, and nothing here lets you:
   the classes are single-typed, so the ordering that would be wrong is one there is no instance for.
-}
pub alias qualified Timestamp = I64 deriving (Eq, Ord)
pub alias qualified Interval = I64 deriving (Eq, Ord)

-- Microseconds, which is what the C++ shows too. It is a diagnostic form and not a presentation
-- one - what a human reads is `DateFormat`'s job - so the bound is `Long`'s twenty and the text is
-- the number.
instance Show(Timestamp):
    fn show(value: Timestamp, &to: String) -> {} = show(value.0 :: Long, to)
    fn showBound(value: Timestamp) -> Maybe(Size) = Just(20)

instance Show(Interval):
    fn show(value: Interval, &to: String) -> {} = show(value.0 :: Long, to)
    fn showBound(value: Interval) -> Maybe(Size) = Just(20)

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The affine operators, as three classes.
   ==========================================================================================

   **§3.1 says "declare the four legal operations as free operators", and that is not a thing this
   language can do.** An overload set is keyed by `(name, arity)` and holds every class function of
   that name and arity plus *at most one* plain function (doc/spec/overloading.md, R1) - so there is
   one plain binary `+` in a program and one plain binary `-`, and `Native` has already spent both
   on pointer arithmetic. That is also why pointer *difference* over there is a named `difference`
   rather than the `-` it wants to be.

   So the alternative §3.1 names in the same sentence and sets aside - "or need three classes" - is
   the one that works, and it is three exactly. `Advance` is a point displaced by a duration,
   `Difference` is the gap between two points, and `Scale` is a duration stretched by a number. Each
   is a real operation with a real name, and between them they admit precisely the seven combinations
   that mean something.

   What makes the two `-`s unambiguous is that a candidate matches or it does not, with no ranking
   (R2): `Timestamp - Interval` finds `Advance(Timestamp)` and no `Difference` candidate, because
   `Difference`'s second argument is a `Timestamp`. `Timestamp - Timestamp` finds the other one for
   the mirror-image reason. And `Timestamp + Timestamp` - the operation this whole design exists to
   refuse - matches nothing at all and is reported as such.

   `Advance` carries the duration as a second parameter with a functional dependency, because a
   `Timestamp` advances by exactly one kind of thing and saying so is what lets `time + amount` be
   typed from `time` alone. `Difference` does not, and the difference between them is a resolver
   rule worth knowing about:

   **an operator in the middle of an infix chain is offered the whole chain's expected type**, which
   is what makes `a `or` b `or` c` at a `WideInt` result compute at 53 bits rather than settling its
   literals to `Int` (test/resolve/LiteralWidth.yana, `adoptChain`). That expected type fills in
   whatever the arguments left open - and a `Difference(t -> i)` leaves `i` open, since no argument
   mentions it. So `later - base == seconds(90)` offered `Bool` as the result of the *subtraction*,
   `i` became `Bool`, and there was no instance of anything at that point. Naming `Interval` in the
   signature closes the hole: nothing is open, so there is nothing for the offer to fill, and the
   class matches on its arguments the way `Num` does. It also says the true thing - a difference
   between two points *in time* is a duration and not a type variable.
-}
pub class Advance(t -> i):
  fn +(point: t, amount: i) -> t
  fn -(point: t, amount: i) -> t

pub class Difference(t):
  fn -(from: t, to: t) -> Interval

pub class Scale(a):
  fn *(amount: a, factor: I64) -> a
  fn /(amount: a, divisor: I64) -> a

instance Advance(Timestamp, Interval):
    fn +(point: Timestamp, amount: Interval) -> Timestamp = Timestamp(point.0 + amount.0)
    fn -(point: Timestamp, amount: Interval) -> Timestamp = Timestamp(point.0 - amount.0)

-- The one instance of this, and the reason there is not one at `Interval`: an interval displaced by
-- an interval is `Advance(Interval)` above, so instancing `Difference(Interval)` as well would give
-- `a - b` on two intervals two matching candidates and make it an error.
instance Difference(Timestamp):
    fn -(from: Timestamp, to: Timestamp) -> Interval = Interval(from.0 - to.0)

-- An interval is its own displacement, which is what makes an interval a vector where a timestamp
-- is a point: `seconds(3) + millis(500)` is a duration, and the two are the same kind of thing.
instance Advance(Interval, Interval):
    fn +(point: Interval, amount: Interval) -> Interval = Interval(point.0 + amount.0)
    fn -(point: Interval, amount: Interval) -> Interval = Interval(point.0 - amount.0)

{-
   Scaling, which `Timestamp` deliberately does not join.

   The C++ has `*` and `/` on a timestamp as well, and both are meaningless: half of a point in time
   is not a point in time, it is half of the displacement from an origin nobody named. A duration is
   the thing with a zero to scale about, so this is instanced there and nowhere else.
-}
instance Scale(Interval):
    fn *(amount: Interval, factor: I64) -> Interval = Interval(amount.0 * factor)
    fn /(amount: Interval, divisor: I64) -> Interval = Interval(amount.0 / divisor)

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Naming a duration, and reading one back.
   ==========================================================================================

   The replacement for `_s` / `_ms` / `_m` / `_hours` / `_days`, and the reason they are functions
   rather than the literal suffixes they port is that this language has no user-defined suffixes -
   which turns out to read better at the call site anyway: `seconds(3)` says its unit in front of
   the number, where `3_s` says it behind.

   Six constructors and six readers, and the readers *truncate* toward zero rather than rounding:
   `toSeconds(millis(1500))` is 1, exactly as an integer division is everywhere else. A caller
   wanting the remainder has `toMicros` and its own arithmetic.
-}
pub fn micros(count: I64) -> Interval = Interval(count)
pub fn millis(count: I64) -> Interval = Interval(count * 1000)
pub fn seconds(count: I64) -> Interval = Interval(count * 1000000)
pub fn minutes(count: I64) -> Interval = Interval(count * 60000000)
pub fn hours(count: I64) -> Interval = Interval(count * 3600000000)
pub fn days(count: I64) -> Interval = Interval(count * 86400000000)

-- Nanoseconds, which is not a unit an `Interval` can hold and is the unit `monotonicNanos` answers
-- in. Here so that the one lossy direction between the two is written once, where it can say that
-- it is lossy, rather than at each call site as a division by a thousand.
pub fn nanos(count: I64) -> Interval = Interval(count / 1000)

pub fn toMicros(amount: Interval) -> I64 = amount.0
pub fn toMillis(amount: Interval) -> I64 = amount.0 / 1000
pub fn toSeconds(amount: Interval) -> I64 = amount.0 / 1000000
pub fn toMinutes(amount: Interval) -> I64 = amount.0 / 60000000
pub fn toHours(amount: Interval) -> I64 = amount.0 / 3600000000
pub fn toDays(amount: Interval) -> I64 = amount.0 / 86400000000

-- The two ways a `Timestamp` and an `Interval` relate to the epoch, which is the one point on the
-- line that has a name. `sinceEpoch` is `time - epoch()` said without the subtraction, and
-- `afterEpoch` is the C++'s `Interval::asTimestamp` under a name that says which direction it goes.
pub fn epoch() -> Timestamp = Timestamp(0)
pub fn sinceEpoch(time: Timestamp) -> Interval = Interval(time.0)
pub fn afterEpoch(amount: Interval) -> Timestamp = Timestamp(amount.0)

-- Microseconds since the epoch, as the way in for a number that came from somewhere else - a
-- `timespec` a kernel filled, a field read out of a file format, a count another program printed.
-- `Timestamp(x)` is the same call and the constructor is public too; what this adds is a name that
-- says which unit it takes, since the one mistake it exists to catch is milliseconds.
pub fn timestampOfMicros(count: I64) -> Timestamp = Timestamp(count)

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Reading a clock.
   ==========================================================================================

   Two, because there are two questions and one answer cannot serve both. `currentTime` is the wall
   clock: it is what "now" means to anything outside this process, it is comparable against a time
   somebody else recorded, and it *steps* when the machine's clock is corrected. `monotonicNanos`
   never steps and has no meaning outside this process, which is what makes it the one to measure
   with and the one that can never be shown to anybody.

   `monotonicNanos` answers an `I64` of nanoseconds rather than an `Interval`, and that is the one
   place in this file where a raw number is the honest type. An `Interval` is microseconds, so
   returning one would throw away three digits from the only clock in the library fine enough to
   time a function call with. `nanos` above converts, at the call site that decides it can afford to.

   Neither can fail. `Native.Clock` is where that is argued: the two clock ids are its own constants
   and the buffer is its own local, so there is no failure a caller could have avoided, and a
   `Result` here would be a case every caller names forever for something that does not happen.
-}
pub fn currentTime() -> Timestamp = Timestamp(unixTimeMicros())

pub fn monotonicNanos() -> I64 = Native.monotonicNanos()

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The calendar.
   ==========================================================================================

   A broken-down date, and the fields are the ones a calendar has rather than the ones a clock does.
   Every field defaults, so `Date {}` is the epoch itself and `Date {year: 2026, month: 8, day: 24}`
   is a date with the time left at midnight - which is what makes the record usable as a literal
   without a constructor per shape.

   **The year is an `I32` where the C++ has an `I16`, and that is what makes `dateOf` total.** A
   `Timestamp` spans about 292 thousand years either side of the epoch; an `I16` covers 32 thousand,
   so a timestamp outside that range broke down into a year that had silently wrapped. Two bytes buys
   a conversion that is defined for every value of its argument, which is worth more than the size of
   a record nobody stores in bulk.

   **The zone is minutes rather than the C++'s quarter-hours.** Its encoding existed to fit an
   offset into a byte, and the two functions that encoded and decoded it were the price. Every zone
   in use is a whole number of minutes in [-720, 840], which an `I16` holds outright, so the pair
   goes and the field means what it says.

   `micros` is 0-999999 and holds *the whole sub-second part*, which the C++'s comment says is
   0-1000 and its code does not: `getDate` writes `timestamp.microseconds % 1000000` into it. The
   name and the range agree here.
-}
pub data Date {
    year: I32 = 1970,
    month: U8 = 1,
    day: U8 = 1,
    hour: U8 = 0,
    minute: U8 = 0,
    second: U8 = 0,
    micros: U32 = 0,
    zoneMinutes: I16 = 0
}

-- Monday first, which is ISO-8601's numbering less one and the C++'s own. Pinned rather than left
-- to declaration order because the numbering is arithmetic here - `weekdayAt` computes the index
-- and reads a constructor out of it - so it is a fact this file depends on rather than one it
-- happens to have.
pub data Weekday
  = @value(0) Monday
  | @value(1) Tuesday
  | @value(2) Wednesday
  | @value(3) Thursday
  | @value(4) Friday
  | @value(5) Saturday
  | @value(6) Sunday

-- A payload-free sum is a number, and its `Show` and its `Enum` instance are generated from the
-- declaration - but `Eq` is not, and comparing two weekdays is the first thing anybody does with
-- one. One line over `valueOf`, which is the reinterpretation the number already is.
instance Eq(Weekday):
    fn ==(lhs: Weekday, rhs: Weekday) -> Bool = valueOf(lhs) == valueOf(rhs)

{-
   Division that rounds *down* rather than toward zero, and the reason both of these exist.

   Every calendar conversion below divides a signed count of microseconds by a positive constant and
   needs the answer that tiles the line evenly: one day before the epoch has to be day -1 with a
   remainder of zero, and truncating division answers day 0 with a remainder of -86400. The C++
   corrects for that afterwards with `if(days < 0) days--`, and that correction is wrong at exactly
   the boundary it was written for - a timestamp that is an exact negative multiple of a day gets
   decremented and its remainder does not, so 1969-12-31T00:00:00 came out as 1969-12-30. Doing the
   division correctly in the first place removes the correction and the case it got wrong.

   `divisor` is positive at every call here, which is what lets both be a sign test on the remainder
   rather than on the pair.

   **Both are written as a correction to `rem` rather than as arithmetic that avoids it, and that is
   about overflow rather than about brevity.** The obvious floored form is `-((divisor - 1 - value) /
   divisor)` for a negative value, and it adds `divisor` to a magnitude that is already the whole
   range: at the most negative `Timestamp` an `I64` holds, `divisor - 1 - value` wraps and the answer
   is off by 292 thousand years. The same trap is one line further down - `value - quotient *
   divisor` is the textbook remainder and `quotient * divisor` is `value` rounded, which at the same
   input is *past* the end of the range. Nothing here multiplies or adds outside the divisor's own
   magnitude, so every value of the argument is a value these are defined at.
-}
fn floorDiv(value: I64, divisor: I64) -> I64:
    let quotient = value / divisor
    return if value `rem` divisor < 0 then quotient - 1 else quotient

-- The remainder that goes with it, and therefore never negative - which `rem` is not, since it takes
-- its sign from the dividend.
fn floorRem(value: I64, divisor: I64) -> I64:
    let remainder = value `rem` divisor
    return if remainder < 0 then remainder + divisor else remainder

let microsPerSecond = 1000000 :: I64
let microsPerDay = 86400000000 :: I64
let secondsPerDay = 86400 :: I64
let secondsPerHour = 3600 :: I64

{-
   The two halves of the civil-calendar conversion, from
   http://howardhinnant.github.io/date_algorithms.html - which is where the C++ took them too.

   The trick both halves share is the *era*: a 400-year block, which is the period over which the
   Gregorian calendar's leap rule repeats exactly and therefore the unit in which day counting has
   no special cases at all. Within an era every quantity below is a bounded non-negative integer and
   every division is exact, which is why there is not a leap-year test anywhere in this file.

   Shifting the year back by one for January and February is the second trick: it puts the leap day
   at the *end* of the shifted year, so the day-of-year formula does not have to know whether the
   February it is walking past had 28 days or 29.
-}
fn daysFromCivil(year: I64, month: I64, day: I64) -> I64:
    let shiftedYear = if month <= 2 then year - 1 else year
    let era = floorDiv(shiftedYear, 400)
    let yearOfEra = shiftedYear - era * 400                                            -- 0-399
    let shiftedMonth = if month > 2 then month - 3 else month + 9                      -- 0-11
    let dayOfYear = (153 * shiftedMonth + 2) / 5 + day - 1                             -- 0-365
    let dayOfEra = yearOfEra * 365 + yearOfEra / 4 - yearOfEra / 100 + dayOfYear       -- 0-146096

    -- 719468 is the number of days from 0000-03-01 - the era's own origin - to 1970-01-01, so
    -- subtracting it is what moves the answer onto the epoch this module counts from.
    return era * 146097 + dayOfEra - 719468

{-
   The whole of `Timestamp` from `Date`, and the one direction that can be asked for a date nobody
   could have observed.

   Nothing here range-checks its argument, which is deliberate and is what makes the function
   useful: month 13 is January of the next year and day 0 is the last day of the previous month,
   which is how date arithmetic is written when there is no calendar type to do it with. The
   formulas above stay exact for any of them.

   The zone is *subtracted*, since a wall-clock reading in a zone ahead of UTC names an earlier
   instant than the same reading in UTC.
-}
pub fn timestampOf(date: Date) -> Timestamp:
    let days = daysFromCivil(date.year :: I64, date.month :: I64, date.day :: I64)
    let secondsOfDay = (date.hour :: I64) * secondsPerHour + (date.minute :: I64) * 60
                     + (date.second :: I64) - (date.zoneMinutes :: I64) * 60

    return Timestamp((days * secondsPerDay + secondsOfDay) * microsPerSecond + (date.micros :: I64))

{-
   And back, which is the half that needs the floor division.

   `civil_from_days` is inlined rather than being a function of its own, exactly as it is in the
   C++: it produces three numbers, this is its only caller, and the alternative is a record that
   exists to be destructured one line later.
-}
pub fn dateOf(time: Timestamp, zoneMinutes: I16 = 0) -> Date:
    let local = time.0 + (zoneMinutes :: I64) * 60 * microsPerSecond
    let days = floorDiv(local, microsPerDay)
    let inDay = floorRem(local, microsPerDay)                                          -- 0-86399999999

    -- 719468 again, moving the epoch-relative day count onto the era's origin so that everything
    -- below is non-negative within its own era.
    let shifted = days + 719468
    let era = floorDiv(shifted, 146097)
    let dayOfEra = shifted - era * 146097                                              -- 0-146096
    let yearOfEra = (dayOfEra - dayOfEra / 1460 + dayOfEra / 36524
                     - dayOfEra / 146096) / 365                                        -- 0-399
    let dayOfYear = dayOfEra - (365 * yearOfEra + yearOfEra / 4 - yearOfEra / 100)     -- 0-365
    let shiftedMonth = (5 * dayOfYear + 2) / 153                                       -- 0-11
    let day = dayOfYear - (153 * shiftedMonth + 2) / 5 + 1                             -- 1-31
    let month = if shiftedMonth < 10 then shiftedMonth + 3 else shiftedMonth - 9       -- 1-12

    -- The shifted year runs March to February, so January and February belong to the next one.
    let year = yearOfEra + era * 400 + (if month <= 2 then 1 else 0)

    let secondsOfDay = inDay / microsPerSecond                                         -- 0-86399

    return Date {
        year: truncate(year) :: I32,
        month: truncate(month) :: U8,
        day: truncate(day) :: U8,
        hour: truncate(secondsOfDay / secondsPerHour) :: U8,
        minute: truncate((secondsOfDay `rem` secondsPerHour) / 60) :: U8,
        second: truncate(secondsOfDay `rem` 60) :: U8,
        micros: truncate(inDay `rem` microsPerSecond) :: U32,
        zoneMinutes: zoneMinutes
    }

{-
   The day of the week, from either end.

   Both go through the day count rather than through a formula of their own, which is what keeps
   there being one calendar in this file: a weekday is a day number modulo seven, and the only thing
   worth writing down is which day the numbering starts on. 1970-01-01 was a Thursday, so adding
   three before the modulus puts Monday at zero.

   `fromValue` is partial because a payload-free sum's numbering is open in general, and the index
   here is provably 0-6 - `floorRem` against a positive divisor cannot answer anything else - so the
   fallback is unreachable rather than a default. It is written as `?? Monday` rather than as a
   seven-arm `match` because the arms would be a second copy of the `@value` list above.
-}
fn weekdayOfDays(days: I64) -> Weekday:
    let named = fromValue(floorRem(days + 3, 7)) :: Maybe(Weekday)
    return named ?? Monday

pub fn weekdayAt(time: Timestamp, zoneMinutes: I16 = 0) -> Weekday:
    let local = time.0 + (zoneMinutes :: I64) * 60 * microsPerSecond
    return weekdayOfDays(floorDiv(local, microsPerDay))

pub fn weekdayOf(date: Date) -> Weekday =
    weekdayOfDays(daysFromCivil(date.year :: I64, date.month :: I64, date.day :: I64))
